# Chroma content pack Art and Writing: FALLS and WRONGS, and the READ EVENT (moments 34 to 39 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md,
# Part 5). Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: library-spec.md and the brief's pack rules. Names in title:,
# grants:, takes:, drops:, requires:, holds: and aims: come from art/catalogue.py, core/reach.py, core/longshot.py and
# the base catalogue. Each life event is even on its own: alpha even, stakes 1.0, the five one-color options plus the
# combination set named in its header comment (34 pairs D6b; 35 to 38 triads D1a, D1b, D2a, D2b). Chances differ by act
# and are set so every color's mean is the same inside each moment (a pair counts half to each of its colors, a triad a
# third), so the file is even in every stage. per_year is the yearly rate of meeting the moment per eligible person
# (whoever holds a title in holds:), estimates unless a source is named; drivers are this file's own reading of what
# moves each rate. Every moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines in the forms of the brief's table (art/worlds.py),
# and an untagged scene for each of the two other worlds; an option whose act reads differently there has its own wording.
# Wrongs are pickable with plain labels: each is marked (hid a wrong, broke your word) and closed by law or approval with
# a real backfire; none is the only way through, and integrity and vanity are spread over the colors (the forgery on
# Blue's craft and Black's fee, the secret on Red's truth-at-any-cost, the borrowed idea on Green's belief that ideas
# pass hand to hand). The fall (38) is plain and offstage: a tremor, stiff joints,
# failing eyes, never more. Drink, drugs and burnout are not the premise of any moment here. No real person, gallery,
# museum, prize, publisher, school, brand, country or year is named.
# The read event (39) follows 'the reviews are in' (stage-events.lib): five readings, one per color, no chance:. The
# brief asked for a cross cycle s1 on it as well; build.py takes exactly five readings on a read event ("a read event
# has five readings, one per color"), and no read event in the Library or the packs carries more, so it has none. Its
# engine condition is READ_ART in engine-conditions.py.

# ================================================================ falls and wrongs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 34. a dealer asks for a painting in a dead master's hand (pairs D6b)
# A wrong that pays well. The forgery sits on the UB pair (Blue's pride in matching a master's hand, Black's fee): it
# grants [savings] when it works, its closed: law line carries the backfire, and a failed try takes [a name in the art
# world] (the forgery is found and the name is gone). The other acts: an honest copy marked as one, a study for the
# studio wall, the offer kept in writing as leverage, a public refusal, the dead painter's family told, the police, a
# warning to the trade, the money talk turned into a show of one's own (a dealer who sells the work, at about a first
# show's real chance, 36), and a tribute under one's own name. Black's single is easy and its BR pair hard, so the pairs
# are solved around the singles until every color's mean is 68.

== a dealer asks for a painting in a dead master's hand
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-95
holds: painter or sculptor | illustrator for hire | gallery owner
per_year: 0 0 .01 .015 .01 .005
drivers: prosper+.2 money-.2
times: per painter, illustrator or gallery owner a year: about 1 in 80 is asked, plainly or with a wink, to make or pass on a picture in a dead master's manner as the master's own; the three careers are about 4 lives in 1,000 (catalogue shares), so about 1 life in 2,000 ever meets it (estimate); museum and auction-house studies find that a real share of the works they test are not by the hand on the label
likelier: a booming art market, a painter with a gift for other hands, debts, a dealer with buyers far away who ask few questions
rarer: a painter with a name of their own, a small town far from the trade, a dealer who has been caught before
gap: 5-15
domain: career.5
tone: trouble
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, friend, colleague, rival
earth: a dealer wants a painting in a dead master's hand, signed with the master's name, for a buyer who will not ask
tribal: a trader in carvings at the gathering wants a carving in a dead master carver's hand, with the dead one's sign cut on it
magic: a picture dealer on the square wants a canvas in a dead court painter's hand, under the dead painter's seal
scene: {boss}, a dealer who has sold a few of {Ns} pictures, closes the door of the back room and opens a catalogue on the table: a small landscape by a painter dead these sixty years. There is a buyer, {boss} says, who wants another one and will pay what a real one fetches. Nobody needs to know who held the brush.
scene W: A signature is a promise about who made the thing. {N} looks at the catalogue and thinks of every buyer who ever trusted one.
scene U: {N} could do it. The dead painter laid in the sky first and dragged the trees wet into it; {N} saw that in a museum years ago and has never forgotten. It would be a fascinating problem, and a crime.
scene B: The fee is more than {N} has earned from painting in three years. {N} weighs the offer, and what {boss} would owe afterwards.
scene R: {Ns} face burns. Years of making work that is truly {Ns} own, and this is what it comes to: a good copy of somebody else.
scene G: The painter in the catalogue is buried in a village churchyard, and the grandchildren still live in the house where the landscapes were painted. {N} thinks of them.
scene tribal: At the great gathering a trader in carvings draws {N} behind the hide shelters and unwraps a small bone horse, cut by a master carver dead these many winters. A trader from the far rivers wants another, and will give a whole canoe of flints for it. Nobody needs to know whose hands cut it.
scene magic: {boss}, a picture dealer with a hall on the square, closes the shutters and unrolls a small canvas by a court painter dead these sixty years. A lord in the north wants another, under the dead painter's seal, and will pay what a true one fetches. Nobody needs to know who held the brush.
- W | paint it as an honest copy, marked as one on the back and the bill | v: conformity, security | tribal: cut it as an honest copy, with your own sign beside the dead master's | magic: paint it as an honest copy, marked as one on the back and the bill of sale | chance: 70
- U | copy the master's sky for the studio wall, only to learn how it was built | mark: learned a skill | habit | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: cut a copy of the master's horse for your own shelter, only to learn how it was made | chance: 70
- B | say no, but keep a written note of the offer to hold over the dealer | v: power, security | tribal: say no, but remember every word of the trader's offer, to use one day | magic: say no, but keep a witnessed note of the offer to hold over the dealer | chance: 85
- R | tell the dealer what the offer is worth, loudly, in front of the customers | identity | v: stimulation, universalism | tribal: tell the trader what the offer is worth, loudly, in front of the whole gathering | chance: 80
- G | tell the dead painter's family what the dealer is planning | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: tell the dead carver's kin what the trader is planning | magic: tell the dead painter's heirs what the dealer is planning | chance: 65
- UB | paint it in the master's hand, sign the dead name, and take the fee | grants: savings | takes_if_fails: a name in the art world | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: forging a dead painter's work and name; backfire: an expert finds a modern pigment under the varnish, the dealer is arrested and names the painter | self_control: - | v: power, achievement | tribal: cut it in the dead master's hand, cut the dead one's sign, and take the flints | magic: paint it in the master's hand, set the dead painter's seal on it, and take the fee | chance: 66
- WU | report the dealer to the police, with a note of every word that was said | mark: made an enemy | identity | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: tell the elders of the gathering every word the trader said | magic: report the dealer to the city watch, with a note of every word that was said | chance: 66
- WG | warn the other painters in town about the dealer's back-room work | mark: helped someone in need | v: universalism, benevolence | tribal: warn the other carvers at the gathering about the trader's offer | magic: warn the painters' guild about the dealer's back-room work | chance: 66
- BR | turn the money talk round, and ask for a show of your own work instead | grants: a dealer who sells your work | aims: painter or sculptor | door | v: power, achievement | tribal: turn the trading talk round, and ask the trader to carry your own carvings instead | magic: turn the money talk round, and ask for a showing of your own work in the dealer's hall | chance: 36
- RG | laugh it off, and paint the dead master a tribute under your own name | habit | v: stimulation, tradition | tribal: laugh it off, and cut the dead master a horse of your own, under your own sign | chance: 76
works: The matter is settled the way {N} meant it to be, and {N} can walk past the dealer's window without looking away.
works: A month later the money, the picture and the trouble are where {N} wanted them, and the studio is quiet again.
fails: Word of the back-room offer gets round the trade, and for a season some doors close to {N} without a reason given.
fails: It goes wrong from the first week, and {boss} makes sure the trade hears {Ns} name in the wrong way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 35. a friend's secret would make the book (triads D1a)
# The wrong is Red's single: the secret put in word for word, because the book is truer with it (broke your word; the
# friend reads it). The others keep it out, disguise it, buy the right to it, give it back as a poem, ask first, own
# up before the book comes out, agree in writing, wait for years, or hand the chapter to the friend to write. Chances
# are set by act (asking a friend's leave depends on the friend; a drawer is in one's own hands) and the singles are
# solved around the triads until every color's mean is 70.

== a friend's secret would make the book
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
holds: novelist | open-mic poet
per_year: 0 0 .02 .02 .02 .01
drivers: ties+.1
times: per novelist or open-mic poet a year: about 1 in 50 finds that the best thing in the book or the poems is a story a friend told in trust (estimate); the two titles are about 1 life in 100 (catalogue shares), so about 1 life in 200 meets it; writers' memoirs and the law reports both show how often a friend or a relative finds themselves in someone's book
likelier: a first book drawn from life, a long friendship, a story nobody else knows, an editor who wants the book to be braver
rarer: a writer of thrillers or fantasy, a friend long dead and without family, a writer who keeps work and friends apart
gap: 4-10
domain: career.5
tone: trouble
life: work, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, boss, partner, colleague
earth: the best chapter of the book is a story a friend told in trust, years ago
tribal: the best part of the long telling is something a friend told at a low fire, years ago, in trust
magic: the best chapter of the book is a story a friend told in trust, years ago, in a tavern corner
scene: The book has been stuck for a year, and then one night {N} sees it: the story {friend} told years ago and has told nobody since, of the months spent leaving the house every morning in a suit after the job was gone. It is the heart the book has been missing. {friend} is still {Ns} closest friend.
scene W: {friend} told it in trust, and said that {N} was the only one who would ever know. A promise does not stop being one because a book needs it.
scene U: Told straight it would be the best chapter {N} has ever written. Changed enough, it might still be; {N} turns over how much a story can be changed before it stops being true.
scene B: An editor has said the book needs one more true, hard thing. This is that thing, and {N} knows exactly what it would do for the book.
scene R: {N} cannot stop writing it. The scene arrives whole, at three in the morning, and it is the truest thing {N} has ever put on a page.
scene G: Thirty years of friendship: the weddings, the funerals, the long walks. {N} thinks of {friend} reading the book in the chair by the window.
scene tribal: The long telling has lacked its heart all winter, and now {N} knows what it is: the thing {friend} told at a low fire years ago, about the hunt where {friend} ran, and has told nobody since. It would hold the whole band silent.
scene magic: The book has stalled for a year, and now {N} sees what it lacks: the story {friend} told years ago in a tavern corner, of the season spent pretending to go to the counting house after the post was lost. It is the heart the book has been missing.
- W | leave it out: a secret told in trust stays a secret | mark: kept your word | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: leave it out of the telling: what was told at a low fire stays there | chance: 77
- U | change it beyond knowing: another town, another age, another life | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: change it beyond knowing: another band, another valley, another winter | magic: change it beyond knowing: another city, another reign, another life | chance: 70
- B | offer the friend a share of the book's money for the right to use it | self_control: + | v: power, achievement | tribal: offer the friend a share of every gift the telling brings, for the right to tell it | chance: 70
- R | put it in word for word, because the book is truer with it | mark: broke your word | closed: approval: a friend's secret put in print; backfire: the friend reads it in the first week, and does not speak to the writer again | self_control: - | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: tell it word for word at the gathering, because the telling is truer with it | chance: 82
- G | write it as one poem for the friend alone, and keep it out of the book | self_control: + | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: make it a verse for the friend alone, and keep it out of the telling | chance: 72
- WUR | put it in, and tell the friend face to face before the book comes out | mark: owned up | closed: approval: a friend's secret put in print without leave, though with warning; backfire: the friend begs for it to come out, and when it stays in, the friendship ends in front of everyone they share | v: universalism, stimulation | tribal: put it in the telling, and tell the friend face to face before the gathering | chance: 55
- WUG | ask the friend first, and cut whatever they ask to be cut | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: ask the friend first, and leave out whatever the friend asks | chance: 65
- WBR | agree in writing with the friend what may go in, and keep to it | binds | v: security, power | tribal: agree with the friend before the elders what may be told, and keep to it | magic: agree under seal with the friend what may go in, and keep to it | chance: 70
- UBG | keep the pages in a drawer, for a book written years from now | self_control: + | v: security, tradition | tribal: keep the story to yourself for many winters, for a telling far ahead | magic: keep the pages locked in a chest, for a book written years from now | chance: 90
- BRG | ask the friend to write that chapter in their own words, under their own name | door | v: benevolence, self-direction | tribal: ask the friend to tell that part at the fire, in the friend's own voice | chance: 50
works: The book goes to the publisher in the spring, and {friend} is at the launch, raising a glass in the second row.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the book is finished at last, and the friendship comes through it.
fails: The book comes out, and a month later a short letter arrives from {friend}, and then nothing for a long time.
fails: The chapter never comes right whichever way {N} turns it, and the book goes back into the drawer.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 36. the idea in someone else's sketchbook (triads D1b)
# The wrong is Green's single: the friend's idea made and let grow as one's own, on the belief that ideas pass from hand
# to hand like seeds (hid a wrong; the friend sees the piece at the show). The others own up to looking, push their own
# idea further, broker the friend's idea, make something wholly new, credit the friend on the label, make it together,
# push the friend to show it, leave a note of praise, or answer it in a piece of their own. Singles solved around the
# triads until every color's mean is 70.

== the idea in someone else's sketchbook
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 17-80
holds: art school student | illustrator for hire | painter or sculptor | street artist
per_year: 0 0 .03 .02 .01 0
drivers: community+.1
times: per art student, illustrator, painter or street artist a year: about 1 in 40 sees in a friend's sketchbook, or on a shared studio wall, the idea they have been circling, done first (estimate); the four titles are about 2 lives in 100 (catalogue shares), so about 1 life in 400 meets it; arguments over who had an idea first are as old as shared studios and art schools
likelier: a shared studio or a crowded art school, a friend a little ahead, a deadline close, a brief that both are working on
rarer: a painter who works alone, a friend with a very different style, the later years of a career
gap: 3-8
domain: career.5
tone: trouble
life: work, friends
horizon: week
roles: friend, mentor, colleague, rival
earth: a friend's sketchbook left open on the studio table, with the idea in it
tribal: a friend's marking stone left by the fire, with a new way of drawing the running deer scratched on it
magic: a friend's sketchbook left open on the academy's long table, with the idea in it
scene: {friend} has gone out for coffee and left the sketchbook open on the studio table. On the page is the idea {N} has been circling for a year, the one that would make the end-of-year piece, done in six quick drawings and better than {N} ever got it.
scene W: {N} should not have looked, and knows it. Whatever happens next, it has to start with that.
scene U: {N} studies the six drawings for a long time. The friend's version is good; it is also only one of the ways the idea could go.
scene B: Nobody saw {N} look. The show is in three weeks, and {friend} works slowly.
scene R: Something between envy and joy goes through {N}. It is a wonderful idea, and it is not {Ns}.
scene G: Ideas drift round a studio like seeds on the wind, and nobody knows whose they were first. Still, it is {Ns} friend's book, open on the table.
scene tribal: {friend} has gone down to the river and left a marking stone by the fire. Scratched on it is a new way of drawing the running deer, the way {N} has tried to find for two winters, and it is right.
scene magic: {friend} has gone out to the bakery and left the sketchbook open on the academy's long table. On the page is the idea {N} has chased all year, done in six quick drawings and better than {N} ever got it.
- W | tell the friend the sketchbook lay open, and what was seen in it | mark: owned up | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: tell the friend the marking stone was seen, and what was on it | chance: 73
- U | push your own idea further, until it no longer needs the friend's | mark: learned a skill | self_control: + | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: work your own marks further, until they no longer need the friend's | chance: 68
- B | tell the friend the idea could sell, and offer to find the buyer | v: achievement, power | tribal: tell the friend the marks could be traded, and offer to find the trader | chance: 75
- R | close the sketchbook, and make something wholly your own that night | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: turn the stone face down, and paint something wholly your own that night | chance: 73
- G | make the piece from the friend's idea, and let it grow as your own | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: an idea taken from a friend's sketchbook; backfire: the friend sees the piece at the show, and the whole studio knows whose idea it was | self_control: - | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: paint the running deer the friend's way, and let the band think it your own | chance: 70
- WUB | make the piece, with the friend's name beside your own on the label | self_control: - | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: paint it, and tell the band at the fire whose marks it grew from | chance: 75
- WRG | ask the friend to make it together, as one piece with both names | mark: made a friend | door | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: ask the friend to paint it together, side by side on the same rock | chance: 60
- WBG | urge the friend to enter it for the open show, and help pay the fee | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, security | tribal: urge the friend to show the marks at the gathering, and carry the friend's paints there | magic: urge the friend to enter it for the academy's open show, and help pay the fee | chance: 65
- URG | leave a note in the sketchbook: this one is good, make it | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: leave a sign of praise scratched beside the friend's marks | chance: 85
- UBR | make an answer to it, and hang both side by side at the show | v: achievement, stimulation | tribal: paint an answer to it, and show both side by side at the gathering | chance: 55
works: By the end of the term the idea has found its way onto a wall, and {N} and {friend} still share a table in the studio.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the show opens with work {N} can stand beside without flinching.
fails: The show opens, and {friend} looks from one wall to the other for a long moment and then leaves early.
fails: Three weeks are not enough, nothing comes right, and {N} hangs an old piece and feels the lack of it all evening.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 37. an advert wants your style (triads D2a)
# Selling out or not, with no verdict either way: the money taken on a tight contract ([savings]), a narrow licence that
# keeps the rights ([royalties]), the fee pushed up ([savings]), a refusal turned into a joke on a wall, the fee given to
# the community centre ([good name in town]); and on the triads an open letter, the neighbours asked, the work passed to a
# younger artist, the advert made into a work that mocks its own product ([following online]), a year bought to paint.
# Singles solved around the triads until every color's mean is 68.

== an advert wants your style
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
holds: street artist | illustrator for hire | painter or sculptor | renowned artist
per_year: 0 0 .03 .03 .02 .01
drivers: prosper+.3 era+.1
times: per street artist, illustrator or painter a year: about 1 in 30 is asked by a firm or its agency to lend their style to an advert, a shop front or a product (estimate); the three careers are about 4 lives in 1,000 (catalogue shares), so about 1 life in 1,000 meets it; advertising has borrowed from street art and illustration for as long as both have existed, sometimes without asking
likelier: a style that has gone round online, a mural in a busy street, good years for advertising, a name in the art world
rarer: a painter known only to a few collectors, a style too strange to sell things, lean years
gap: 3-8
domain: career.5
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, friend, colleague, neighbour
earth: a big firm wants an artist's style for a year of adverts
tribal: a trading clan from downriver wants an artist's painted patterns on every pot it trades
magic: a merchant house wants an artist's style on its shop signs and its printed bills for a year
scene: The email comes from an agency: a big firm that makes soft drinks wants {Ns} style for a year of adverts, on the sides of buses and on screens in the stations. The walls, the lettering, the faces: all of it, in {Ns} hand. The fee is more than {N} has earned in the last two years together.
scene W: A contract is only as good as what it says. {N} reads the attachment twice, looking for where the work would end up, and for how long.
scene U: {N} can see three ways to answer the brief, and one way to turn it inside out so that the advert becomes something else entirely.
scene B: The figure in the email is real money. {N} wonders what the firm would pay if asked, and what the name would be worth after a year on every bus.
scene R: The style came from nights on cold walls with friends, painting what the street needed to see. {N} reads the email aloud to {friend} and they both start laughing.
scene G: {N} learned to paint on the shutters of the street where {N} grew up. The neighbours still stop to look at those walls. {N} thinks of them seeing the same faces selling soft drinks.
scene tribal: A trading clan from downriver has sent its headman with gifts: the clan wants {Ns} painted patterns on every pot it trades, so the bands downriver will know the clan's pots by them. The gifts are more than {Ns} hearth has had in two winters.
scene magic: A merchant house has sent its factor with a letter: it wants {Ns} style on its shop signs and its printed bills for a year, in every town on the river. The fee is more than {N} has earned from painting in two years.
- W | say yes, on a contract listing every place the work may appear | grants: savings | v: conformity, security | tribal: agree, but only for the pots the clan names before the elders | magic: say yes, on a sealed contract listing every place the work may appear | chance: 66
- U | license the style for one year and one product, and keep every right | grants: royalties | v: achievement, security | tribal: let the clan use the patterns for one summer and one kind of pot, and keep the rest | magic: license the style to the merchant house for one year and one ware | chance: 73
- B | take the money, and push the fee up before signing | grants: savings | identity | v: power, achievement | tribal: take the clan's gifts, and ask for more before agreeing | magic: take the merchant's gold, and push the fee up before signing | chance: 71
- R | turn it down, and paint the offer letter into the next wall as a joke | mark: turned down a chance | v: stimulation, hedonism | tribal: turn it down, and paint the trading clan as fat ducks on the shelter wall | magic: turn it down, and paint the merchant's letter into the next wall as a joke | chance: 74
- G | take it, and give the fee to the community centre where it all started | grants: good name in town | identity | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: take the gifts, and share them out among the band's old ones | magic: take it, and give the fee to the ward's hall where it all started | chance: 66
- WUR | say no, and write an open letter about who owns a style | identity | v: universalism, self-direction | tribal: say no, and tell every fire whose patterns these are | magic: say no, and post a letter on the guild hall door about who owns a style | chance: 65
- WUG | ask the neighbours who helped paint the walls, and do what they decide | door | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: ask the band whose shelter walls taught the patterns, and do what it decides | magic: ask the quarter whose walls taught the style, and do what it decides | chance: 70
- WBG | send the firm to a younger artist who needs the work more | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, security | tribal: send the trading clan to a younger painter who needs the gifts more | magic: send the merchant house to a younger painter who needs the work more | chance: 75
- UBR | make the advert a work of its own that quietly mocks the product | grants: following online | mark: took a wild risk | v: stimulation, achievement | tribal: paint the pots so the patterns quietly laugh at the clan that trades them | magic: make the shop sign a work of its own that quietly mocks the ware | chance: 55
- BRG | take the money, and buy a year to paint nothing but your own work | binds | v: self-direction, hedonism | tribal: take the gifts, and spend a whole winter painting only what you choose | magic: take the gold, and buy a year to paint nothing but your own work | chance: 65
works: The year turns out the way {N} wanted, and the work, whoever paid for it, still looks like {Ns} own.
works: Whatever {N} chose, {N} can look the neighbours in the eye when the buses go past.
fails: The firm's lawyers rewrite everything at the last minute, and the year goes sour in a slow, expensive way.
fails: The agency goes to another artist with a style a little like {Ns}, and by the summer it is on every bus anyway.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 38. hands that will not hold the brush (triads D2b)
# A plain fall, told plainly and kept offstage: a tremor, stiff joints, eyes that blur at close work. The answers: the
# treatment followed, the method changed, the name turned into prints and licences ([royalties]), the shake let into the
# work, the end of one chapter (drops the career), teaching ([pupils who remember you]), the workshop given away (drops
# the career), the last commissions finished as promised, the other hand learned, an assistant taught. Singles solved
# around the triads until every color's mean is 66. The brief's per_year has .005 for young adults, but the moment's
# stages and its ages (40 to 100) leave no young adult to meet it, and build.py refuses a rate outside the stages: 0.

== hands that will not hold the brush
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 40-100
holds: painter or sculptor | master craftsperson | illustrator for hire | craft maker
per_year: 0 0 0 .02 .04 .06
drivers: health-.5 stress+.1
times: per painter, illustrator or maker a year: about 1 in 25 in later life finds the hands or the eyes failing the fine work (a tremor, arthritis in the fingers, cataracts or a macular change); the four careers are about 1 life in 100 (catalogue shares), so about 1 life in 300 meets it (estimate); hand osteoarthritis and essential tremor both become common after sixty
likelier: old age, decades of fine close work, a family history of tremor, a hard winter in a cold studio
rarer: middle age, work on a large scale with the whole arm, good health, a maker who has always worked with assistants
gap: 5-15
domain: career.5
tone: trouble
life: work, health
horizon: years
roles: partner, colleague, mentor, friend, child
earth: the hands shake, or stiffen, and the fine work will not come
tribal: the carver's hands stiffen in the cold, and the fine lines will not come
magic: the guild painter's hands begin to shake, and the fine work will not come
scene: It starts with the fine work: the line that will not stay where it is put, the knuckles that ache by noon, the brush that slips. The specialist is kind and plain about it. It will not get better, though it may get worse slowly, and there are things that help.
scene W: There is a way to meet this, as there is a way to do everything: the treatment, the exercises, the hours kept. {N} means to do it properly.
scene U: {N} watches the hand like a problem to be solved: which tools it can still hold, which movements still come clean, what larger marks might do that small ones cannot.
scene B: {N} has spent thirty years building a name. The question is how to keep it working when the hands will not.
scene R: {N} throws the brush across the studio, and then laughs, because the mark it leaves on the wall is wilder than anything painted all year.
scene G: Every maker {N} has known came to this in the end, the old masters too. {N} sits a long time at the bench in the afternoon light, thinking of the work that is already done.
scene tribal: Since the long cold the carver's fingers will not close round the fine flint, and the lines of the antlers come out wrong. The old ones nod; it came to their own hands too, in the end.
scene magic: The guild painter's hands have begun to shake at the fine work, and the physicians' hall has no cure for it, only salves and patience.
- W | see the specialist, and follow the treatment to the letter | self_control: + | habit | v: conformity, security | tribal: go to the healer, and keep every rule of the cure | magic: go to the physicians' hall, and follow the cure to the letter | chance: 64
- U | change the method: longer brushes, a strap at the wrist, a new material | mark: learned a skill | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: change the way of working: a longer stick, a thong at the wrist, a softer stone | chance: 72
- B | turn the name into prints, licences and a shop of the old work | grants: royalties | habit | v: power, achievement | tribal: trade the old carvings far and wide while the name still carries | magic: sell prints and copies of the old work while the name still sells | chance: 67
- R | work with the shaking hand, and let the shake into the work | mark: took a wild risk | self_control: + | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 67
- G | put the tools down for good, and take it as the end of one chapter | drops: painter or sculptor; master craftsperson; illustrator for hire; craft maker | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: lay the tools down for good, and take it as the turn of a season | chance: 65
- WUB | teach at the college two days a week, and pass on the method | grants: pupils who remember you | door | v: benevolence, achievement | tribal: teach the young ones the ways of the hand, two days in every moon | magic: teach at the academy two days a week, and pass on the method | chance: 60
- WRG | give the tools and the workshop to a young maker who needs them | drops: painter or sculptor; master craftsperson; illustrator for hire; craft maker | mark: helped someone in need | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: give the tools and the carving place to a young carver of the band | magic: give the tools and the workshop to a young guild maker who needs them | chance: 80
- WBR | finish the last commissions as promised, whatever it costs the hands | mark: kept your word | self_control: + | v: conformity, power | tribal: finish the carvings promised to the traders, whatever it costs the hands | chance: 65
- URG | learn to draw with the other hand, slowly, like a child again | habit | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: learn to cut with the other hand, slowly, like a child again | chance: 50
- UBG | take on an assistant, and teach them every step while there is time | grants: pupils who remember you | binds | v: tradition, achievement | tribal: take a young helper, and show every step while there is time | magic: take on an apprentice, and teach every step while there is time | chance: 70
works: A year on, the work has found a new shape, smaller or larger or in other hands, and {N} has made a kind of peace with it.
works: Whatever {N} chose, the studio is not a sad place, and people still come to it.
fails: The months go by in waiting rooms and bad days, and {N} cannot settle to anything.
fails: The plan does not hold, the hand gets worse faster than anyone said, and {N} has to begin again from somewhere else.

# ================================================================ the read event

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 39. the reviews of the show are in (read event: five readings)
# What the maker reads the morning after the private view, on every visual road. As in 'the reviews are in'
# (stage-events.lib): tone mixed and one small gain on every reading, so the readings net out even; the hard readings (a
# price list, a wound) carry a lower impact and a small gain of their own (autonomy, the warmth of the other painters).
# No chance: on readings. The engine's condition is READ_ART in engine-conditions.py (someone whose work was just shown);
# holds: names the same five titles as its req.

== the reviews of the show are in
tier: read
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
holds: painter or sculptor | renowned artist | street artist | illustrator for hire | master craftsperson
source: career
tone: mixed
base: need:competence-.03
earth: the papers and the online notices the morning after the private view
tribal: the talk at every fire after the carvings are shown at the gathering
magic: the broadsheets cried in the square the morning after the showing in the guild hall
times: per painter, street artist, illustrator or master craftsperson whose work is shown: once a show; most shows in a city gallery are noticed somewhere, and many small ones get a line in the local paper or a post online (estimate)
window: 17-100
gap: 1-3
likelier: a first show of one's own, a gallery in a city, a critic at the private view, a name in the art world
rarer: a group show in a village hall, a week full of bigger news, work shown only online
scene: The morning after the private view the notices are in: a short piece in the city paper, a long one on an art website, and three lines in the local weekly. {N} reads them at the kitchen table, with the invitation card still propped against the kettle.
scene tribal: Traders from three bands have walked past the new carvings on the gathering ground, and by evening every fire has its opinion: the old carvers grunt, the young ones scratch the horses into the dust, and one elder says it plainly to {Ns} face.
scene magic: The morning after the showing in the guild hall, the broadsheets are cried in the square: two kind, one cruel, and a long letter from a gentleman of the academy. {N} reads them in the window seat of the bakery opposite.
- W | a show hung and a promise kept: the work was honest, whatever they wrote | impact: 1.0 | also: meaning+.05 | say: {N} reads the notices once, puts them in the folder with the price list, and is at the gallery at ten to meet the school party, as arranged.
- U | a lesson: one critic saw the weak corner of the big canvas | impact: 0.9 | also: competence+.05 | say: {N} underlines the sharp sentence about the big canvas, goes back to the studio, and repaints the corner it was right about.
- B | a price list: a good line sells three pictures, a bad one sells one | impact: 0.6 | also: autonomy+.05 | say: {N} rings the gallery before reading a word, asks how many pictures have sold, and has the best line printed on the card for the next show.
- R | a wound: one sentence that will sting for years | impact: 0.6 | also: belonging+.05 | say: {N} reads the cruel sentence until it is learned by heart, then goes to the pub with the other painters from the show, and by closing time they have made a song of it.
- G | the way of things: some seasons the work is loved, and some it is not | impact: 0.9 | also: safety+.05 | say: {N} cuts the kind paragraph out of the local paper for the family album, leaves the rest where it lies, and goes out to the garden.
